  "body": "<ul><li><p><strong>Check the sponge bar</strong><br>If the sponge bar is flat, compressed, or worn, it won’t hold the needles forward correctly. When needles don’t stay in position, they may fail to knit stitches consistently.</p></li><li><p><strong>Confirm the yarn is machine-appropriate</strong><br>Yarn that’s too thick or too stiff for your machine’s needle size can prevent stitches from forming properly. If in doubt, try a thinner, smoother yarn as a test.</p></li><li><p><strong>Watch for repeat offenders</strong><br>Does the problem happen with the same needles every time? Bent, sticky, or damaged needles are common culprits. Swapping a few suspect needles is a quick way to confirm.</p></li><li><p><strong>Consider overall machine condition</strong><br>If none of the above resolves the issue—especially if problems are widespread—your machine may need professional servicing.</p></li></ul><p></p>",
